{
 "result": "primes.py is built and verified: it prints the count of primes below int(argv[1]) using a mod-30 wheel sieve over 8 residue bytearrays, presieved by pattern replication (primes 7..17, plus 19 when n >= 2e7), with a mod-6 wheel fallback for n < 1000. Correctness: `python3 primes.py 5000000` prints 348513; verify.py reports OK against an independent odd-only reference sieve for every n in 0..4999, 40 random n up to 3e6, values around 510510*k (the presieve period), 2e7 and 21234567, plus the known counts for 5e6, 3e7 (1857859) and 1e8 (5761455); spot checks at prime boundaries (999983, 1000003, 1000004, 29999999, 99999989) also matched. Speed: on `python3 assay.py` (times 5e6, 3x1e8 and 3e7, prints 100/dt) the registered baseline was 138.7 (odd-only sieve), the mod-6 wheel scored about 235-240, and the current version scored 334.91, 291.25 and 327.38 on three runs after the merge, roughly 2.1-2.4x the baseline. Those runs spread about 13%, matching the assay's noise, so the exact multiple is loose. A numpy variant was tried and was slower here because its import cost outweighs the savings at n <= 1e8. Leftover scratch files (cA.py, cB.py, cC.py, p6.py, pnp.py, _*.txt, backups) remain in the folder and are not needed by primes.py.",
